<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · river</p><h1 class="enc-title">Little Zab</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Lesser Zab, Lower Zab</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Little Zab is a river that rises in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zagros-mountains">Zagros</a> mountains of north-western <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iran">Iran</a> and flows south-west through Iraqi <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kurdistan">Kurdistan</a> to join the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tigris">Tigris</a>. With the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/great-zab">Great Zab</a> to the north it framed the Assyrian heartland around <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/erbil">Arbela</a>, later the Kingdom of Adiabene, while ancient states such as Arrapha around <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kirkuk">Kirkuk</a> and the hill peoples of Simurrum lay near its course. The Dokan Dam now holds back its waters.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/little-zab.jpg" alt="Little Zab" width="320" height="212" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Osama Shukir Muhammed Amin FRCP(Glasg) · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Lake_Dukan,_Sulaymaniyah_Governorate,_Iraqi_Kurdistan.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>The Little Zab, or Lower Zab, is called al-Zāb al-Asfal in Arabic, Zêy Koya in Kurdish and Zāba Taḥtāya in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/syriac">Syriac</a>. It joins the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tigris">Tigris</a> just south of Al Zab in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iraq">Iraq</a>. Estimates of its length range from 380 to 456 kilometres, and it drains about 22,000 square kilometres, 74% of it in Iraq. It is fed by rainfall and snowmelt, with peak discharge from February to May and low water from July to October. Because of its torrential nature, medieval Arab geographers described it, like the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/great-zab">Great Zab</a>, as demoniacally possessed. Annual precipitation falls from over 1,000 millimetres in the Iranian Zagros to under 200 at its confluence with the Tigris, and the tree line stands at about 1,800 metres.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>Human occupation of the basin is attested for every period since the Middle Palaeolithic site of Barda Balka, where Late Acheulean stone tools were found. Jarmo, a tell east of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kirkuk">Kirkuk</a>, was a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/neolithic">Neolithic</a> village of farmers and herders, and the Ranya Plain was occupied from the Ubaid to the Ninevite V periods, from the middle of the 6th to the middle of the 3rd millennium BCE. The region enters history at the end of the 3rd millennium BCE, when <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shulgi">Shulgi</a> of the Ur III dynasty mentions <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/erbil">Erbil</a> as Urbilum.</p><p>In the early 2nd millennium BCE Shamshi-<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hadad">Adad</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/upper-mesopotamia">Upper Mesopotamia</a> fought in the land of Qabra, probably on the lower Little Zab, and installed garrisons. Tablets from Tell Shemshara, ancient Shusharra, show the local governor becoming his <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vassal">vassal</a>. In the 14th century BCE the region belonged to the kingdom of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mitanni">Mitanni</a>, with archives at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nuzi">Nuzi</a> and Tell al-Fakhar. The lower basin lay in the heartland of the Middle and Neo-Assyrian empires. After the Neo-Assyrian fall control passed to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/medes">Medes</a> and, in 550 BCE, to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/achaemenid-empire">Achaemenid Empire</a>. Alexander the Great defeated <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/darius-iii">Darius III</a> at the Battle of Gaugamela, and after Alexander's death in 323 BCE the area fell to his Seleucid successors.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>Two dams regulate the river in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iraq">Iraq</a>: the Dukan Dam, built between 1957 and 1961 as an arch dam 116 metres high that stores water for irrigation and generates hydroelectric power, and the Dibis Dam, built between 1960 and 1965 for the Kirkuk Irrigation Project. Because Lake Dukan would submerge archaeological sites, surveys and rescue excavations were carried out, notably at Tell Shemshara and Tell Bazmusian. For a short distance the river forms the border between <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iran">Iran</a> and Iraq, and on its lower course it divides the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/governorate">governorates</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/erbil">Erbil</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sulaymaniyah">Sulaymaniyah</a> and Kirkuk.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Little Zab” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Hamazi is known only from a handful of texts, and even its location is uncertain; most scholars place it in the Zagros foothills between the Diyala and the <b>Lesser Zab</b>, perhaps near modern Sulaymaniyah.</blockquote><a href="/state/hamazi">Hamazi</a></li><li><blockquote>Adiabene lay between the Great and <b>Lesser Zab</b> rivers east of the Tigris, the heartland of ancient Assyria.</blockquote><a href="/state/kingdom-of-adiabene">Kingdom of Adiabene</a></li><li><blockquote>Its kings ruled the plain between the <b>Lesser Zab</b> and the Zagros foothills as Mitannian vassals.</blockquote><a href="/state/kingdom-of-arrapha">Kingdom of Arrapha</a></li><li><blockquote>Simurrum lay on the routes between Mesopotamia and the Iranian highlands, probably near the <b>Lesser Zab</b> or the Sirwan (upper Diyala); its capital has not been securely located.</blockquote><a href="/state/simurrum">Simurrum</a></li></ul></section>
